WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:08.280
Continuing our quest for solid error handling,&nbsp;
whereas a try statement attempts to run code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:15.000
the except statement runs code if the code being&nbsp;
run in the try statement causes a runtime error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.880
Continuing from the previous video, I have&nbsp;
added, to this 522-except file, which shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.840
a list of figuratives for a language, an except&nbsp;
block on lines 5 and 6. Within the except block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.680
we see a print statement telling the person&nbsp;
running this app to try again by entering a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.480
number for a figurative. We did not try to run&nbsp;
the code in the previous video because a try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.800
block will not run without an except block, as we&nbsp;
have now, or a finally block, which we will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:53.120
in an upcoming topic. I will run this code and&nbsp;
enter a legitimate value. There was no output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.840
as we will add the code for that in the next&nbsp;
video, but there was no error, which is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:08.000
we anticipated. I will rerun the code and&nbsp;
enter an illegitimate value, and this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.440
we get the error message that says we did not&nbsp;
enter a figurative, so the except block worked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:26.360
In other words, this message looks a lot better&nbsp;
than this message. Before we proceed, let's take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:33.840
moment to remind ourselves what an exception is in&nbsp;
Python. An exception is an event that disrupts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.000
normal flow of the program, often due to some kind&nbsp;
of error or exceptional condition. Exceptions are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.360
types of runtime errors, but they can be handled&nbsp;
gracefully using mechanisms like try, except,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:51.720
finally, and else blocks, allowing the program to&nbsp;
continue or terminate in a controlled way. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.800
the purpose of an except block is to handle an&nbsp;
exception gracefully and control the message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.440
given when a runtime error occurs, perhaps&nbsp;
even to provide instructions on what to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:08.600
to avoid the error in the future, as we have&nbsp;
here with instructions in the except block.

